it depends there is no one best way but  what we do know is that most folks  overwhelmingly think that the right  answer that question is that he who  speaks first or she who speaks first is  lost and that is not the right answer  it depends on where you get the biggest  bang for your effort because when you  make an offer you get the benefit of  anchoring your counterpart setting a  starting point to which they must  respond if you receive the first offer  you get the benefit of the information  and so you're in an information  advantage position so the question is  which of those two in the particular  negotiation you're facing is going to be  more powerful where is the benefit is  the benefit from setting the anchor or  is the benefit from getting the  information one piece of the answer to  that is how prepared are you for the  negotiation and another important piece  is how prepared is your counterpart  because that will begin to give you  insight into which of the strategies is  the right one for a particular  negotiation  in the course of the negotiation you're  going to be trading proposals back and  forth your counterpart is gonna want to  go issue by issue this is such a common  belief right we even have a name for it  solve the easy issues first this is a  bad idea it appears at first that it  might be good right you solve the easy  issues now there's a commitment that  each of you has so it's harder to walk  away but if you solve all the easy  issues what's left the really ugly  800-pound gorilla in the room and you  have nothing left to trade but there's  something even more insidious than that  solve the easy issues first assumes that  you're easy issues are my easy issues  what if they're not what if my easy  issue turns out to be a really important  and critical issue for you and because  I'm using that simple rule solve the  easy issues first we get agreement on  that issue  I've lost a degree of freedom I've lost  the capacity to use that concession to  get something that I care about and that  is a real failure on my part what's  going to happen in negotiation is I want  you to focus on negotiating at the  package level not at the issue level and  then once you have that package what's  going to happen is your counterparts are  going to try to break it apart to go  issue by issue you need to resist that  in fact one way to do that is to keep  talking about the issues as a package  and then use what I call yoking language  if this happens if I make a concession  here then I get this concession here you  keep trying to bring it back to the  package level because your counterparts  will try because they believe it's  easier to negotiate issue by issue if  you negotiate issue by issue you have a  series of battles and everybody is  keeping score if you negotiate at the  package level  it's a proposal that we can craft to  make us both better off to find that  solution space where I'm better off and  your whole or better off but it's the  package that does that otherwise we're  just going through one battle after  another one of the most important things  that you can figure out is when should  you walk away  most folks go into a negotiation and do  not have a clear idea about when the  deal moves from being a deal they could  consider - when the deal becomes one  they should run from and that's what  preparation is really about so you need  to know what your alternatives are what  happens to you if there were no  agreement what's your bottom line what's  the point of indifference between saying  yes and walking away and then something  that most people don't think about  what's your aspiration what is an  optimistic assessment of what you could  achieve in this negotiation and that is  so important and so many people don't  even consider what's possible
 

